# A Guided Laboratory for Concentration and Spectral Transfer

The three-dimensional [Navier–Stokes equations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Navier%E2%80%93Stokes_equations) combine viscous smoothing with nonlinear transport. The global smoothness question remains open, but two exact pieces of its surrounding mathematics can be explored directly: an epsilon-regularity reduction and conservative transfer among finitely many modes.

## 1. Look for one good scale

An excess $E(z,r)$ measures local behavior at point $z$ and positive scale $r$. If an epsilon-regularity theorem is available, a single inequality $E(z,r)<arepsilon$ certifies regularity at $z$. Try the threshold explorer:

