{{visualization:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the logical proof</summary>
Assume $z$ is singular. If some positive radius obeyed $E(z,r)<arepsilon$, epsilon regularity would make $z$ regular, a contradiction. Therefore $E(z,r)\gearepsilon$ for every $r>0$. Calling these persistent points $C$ and the singular set $S$ gives $S\subseteq C$. By monotonicity of any measure, $\mu(C)=0$ implies $\mu(S)=0$.
</details>

For background on the geometric notion used in classical partial regularity, see [Hausdorff measure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hausdorff_measure).

## 2. See containment become nullity

The next widget constructs finite pictures where every singular point is concentrated. Finite dots are only an analogy for measurable sets, but they make the subset logic visible.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

<details><summary>Definitions and caveat</summary>
The concentration set consists of points whose excess stays at least $arepsilon$ at every positive scale. The theorem transfers a null estimate from concentration to singularity; it does not itself prove the concentration set null, nor does it prove the singular set empty.
</details>

## 3. Audit a nonlinear energy exchange

For modal states $u_i$ and nonlinear interactions $N_i$, define $	au_i=\langle N_i,u_iangle$. Transfer into a band $A$ is $T(A)=\sum_{i\in A}	au_i$. If $T(I)=0$, finite additivity gives $T(I\setminus A)=-T(A)$.

