{{visualization:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Why conservation does not dictate a cascade</summary>
The identity permits $T(A)>0$, $T(A)<0$, or $T(A)=0$. It forces only the opposite value in the complement. A preferred forward or inverse cascade needs extra dynamical or statistical assumptions. Learn more about the physical picture at [energy cascade](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_cascade).
</details>

## 4. Build test cases and inspect the exchange

A balancing mode can be appended to arbitrary tentative transfer rates by setting its transfer to minus their sum. This creates exact conservative test data in linear time.

